# Line endings are decided HERE, not by whatever Git or editor each machine happens to have.
#
# Why this file exists. On 29-sep the repo showed 11 modified files with ZERO content
# changes: `.editorconfig`, `.gitignore`, `.prettierrc`, `README.md`, `proxy.conf.json`,
# `src/app/api/Health/*`, `src/main.ts` and the three `tsconfig*.json`. Every line was
# deleted and re-added identically. Nobody had edited them — Visual Studio saves with CRLF,
# the committed blobs are LF, and with no rule here Git calls that a rewrite of the file.
#
# It is not cosmetic. Phantom diffs hide the real change in review, and they turn into merge
# conflicts on lines nobody touched.
#
# `* text=auto eol=lf` says: let Git detect what is text, store it LF in the repository, and
# check it out LF on every platform. The companion half of the fix is `end_of_line = lf` in
# .editorconfig — without it the editor simply writes the CRLF back and the churn returns.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts are the exception: cmd.exe misparses an LF-only .bat.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# Text that must stay LF even though the extension is unusual.
*.svg  text eol=lf

# Binary: never normalized, never diffed as text. Without these, a `text=auto` misdetection
# corrupts a font or an image by "fixing" bytes that happen to look like newlines.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.avif  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
